import numpy as np
DESCRIPTION = """h_t = relu(x_t*W_x + h_(t-1)@W_h), h_0 = 0, out = h_10 @ W_o; the decision (largest of the ten scores)
is the position of the second largest of the ten inputs (95% agreement; the rest are near-ties).
The state after step 1 is exactly 2-dimensional: h1 = pos1*max(W_x,0) + neg1*max(-W_x,0), pos1 = x1^+, neg1 = x1^-.
The state after step 2 is a function of only three numbers (pos1, neg1, x2), so it lives in a low-dimensional
subspace: 71%, 25%, 3% of its variance is in its first three principal directions. The same is true of step 3.
So at steps 2 and 3 the construction names the coordinates of the state in the leading principal directions of its
reachable set (computed from a fixed synthetic sample, not from the weights), and rebuilds the site from them; from
step 4 on the whole 16-vector state is carried and the one-step rule rebuilds each later site.
Units 0,2,3,5,10..15 have almost no input weight and only integrate the state, while units 4 and 1,6,7,8 have
large input weights of opposite sign, so |x_t| enters through two opposing groups. The readout is linear in h_10."""

